            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house is already quite airtight. During the blower door test, I identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the exterior doors where there may be wear or damage to the weatherstripping



** The current chimney for the propane fireplace



Savings: I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss is estimated to save $63 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.9 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding. I also recommend extending the new insulation down to ground level, and will discuss this in the basement section of this report. 



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding a minimum R15 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $710 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the DHERP. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modeled extending my recommendation to add 3 inches (R15) rigid foam polystyrene insulation to your main walls down to grade level, adding insulation to the outside of your basement walls. 



Savings: By adding R15 to your basement walls as described above, you could potentially save $460 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $238.75 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

Most of the windows in your house have already been upgraded. However, there is one older window in your basement that is an older double pane window that could be upgraded. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing the older window in your basement with one that meets the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $2 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.1 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <doors>

Both of the interior garage doors in this house are older wood doors. The one to the main floor of your house is a solid wood door with no window, and the one to the basement is a hollow wood door with no window. As the garage is heated but kept at a lower temperature than the rest of your house, I recommend you upgrade both of these older doors to ENERGY STAR?????? rated insulated doors for our climate zone if possible.



Savings: By replacing these 2 older doors with ones that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified for our climate zone, you could potentially save $145 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, you mentioned that you were experiencing high humidity in the winter. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



You already have a passive ventilation system in your home. I recommend that you replace this with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 55% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. 



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, with a minimum 55% efficiency, your energy costs could increase by $210 per year in energy costs and your annual energy usage could increase by 5.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



I also noticed that the combustion air supply is covered by a bucket and partly taped up. I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler are satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electronic damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler is operating. However, if you upgrade your boiler to a direct vent model, the combustion air duct will no longer be required and can be sealed. 

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. Please note that the energy savings in GJ listed above is for an oil boiler. 



As the hot water is heated using the boiler, if you are looking to reduce your greenhouse gas footprint, I suggest setting the hot water to run off the boiler in the winter, and switching to electricity in the summer. By contrast, using the boiler to heat the hot water over the summer would save your approximately $200 per year, even considering short cycling. 



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with an 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $2,780 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 62.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



By contrast, replacing your current oil boiler with a new 87% oil boiler will save you $1,520 per year, decrease your annual energy usage by 43.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



Regardless of whether you choose to switch to propane or stay on oil, while updating your boiler you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 5 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 34.9 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil boiler vents up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting. If you install a woodstove, you should also install a direct vent appliance with a dedicated combustion air duct, and be aware that running all your exhaust fans when the woodstove is lit may cause combustion products to be drawn into the living room.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a 360 CFM range hood   

        - three bathroom fans and one utility fan in your laundry room

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



You could also consider adding a humidistat to your fans upstairs as a temporary solution to some of the moisture issues you mentioned during my site visit. A humidstat will turn the fan on when the humidity upstairs is higher than desired, and run the fan until the humidity reaches the desired set point. 



